  "query_preview": "..\nLight rain will slowly move northeast into the region tomorrow \nafternoon and linger overnight as it pushes east. Most locations \nlook to have a damp and chilly Halloween evening. Fog will \ncontinue to impact portions of the region again tonight into \ntomorrow morning. The most likely impacted areas will be along \nriver valleys. Fog could become locally dense, reducing visibility\nin spots to 2 mile or less.\n\nMeanwhile, as the upper ridge axis shifts northeast, a new \nPacific trough will gain influence late in the weekend into early \nnext week. Southwest flow aloft in advance of the trough with an \napproaching shortwave will lead to breezy winds and increased \nwidespread precipitation chances late Sunday as surface low across\nWyoming tracks into the Dakotas. Models continue to trend higher \ntotals toward favoring southeast Montana, including Glendive and \nSidney. No snow is expected at the time of this forecast update.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNo changes to NBM this cycle. \n\nA lot of uncertainty regarding fog overnight. There's very little\nmodel agreement in location, timing, and intensity. I have the \nmost confidence in areas that saw fog last night/this morning \nseeing fog again overnight. Further more, model agreement after\nWednesday is noticeably low.",
